\<div>[class]{baux-scrub-bar}[aria-live]{polite}{OxCaml 5.2.0+ox (N = 4,096)  ·  Canonical 56 median: 1.59× (pipeline 1.80×, max 2.60×)  ·  Scalable D=64 max: 10.05× (perm-zero, 210× vs Python)}
\<figcaption>[class]{tcv-fig-caption}{Empirical Apple Silicon throughput across the 56-function \code{OxCaml 5.2.0+ox} benchmark suite (\code{float#} unboxed scalars, \code{float64x2#} ARM64 NEON + \code{SLEEF 3.9.0} 2-lane SIMD, work-stealing domains, #{N = 4{,}096} candidates, 12+ adaptive \code{Core_bench} samples #{\times\, 3} repeats) and the zero-\code{Py_INCREF} modal CPython 3.14 extension (\code{_baux_oxcaml}). \strong{(a)} Speedup over sequential scalar \code{float#} across the 6 canonical families (median \code{1.59×} \code{Batch.run}, \code{1.80×} end-to-end pipeline, up to \code{2.60×} on \code{de-jong5} and \code{2.63×} pipeline on \code{forrester}) and all 18 scalable functions at #{D = 16} and #{D = 64} (\code{10.05×} on \code{perm-zero}, \code{9.72×} on \code{perm}, \code{5.12×} on \code{michalewicz}, \code{4.07×} on \code{griewank}). On 5-FLOP algebraic surfaces at #{D = 64} (\code{sphere}, \code{powell}), pre-packed \code{Batch.run} achieves \code{1.64×–2.23×} while unamortized one-shot \code{Batch.init + Batch.run} measures \code{0.79×–0.94×} due to #{4{,}096 \times 64} coordinate pair-interleaving bandwidth. \strong{(b)} Multicore throughput scaling (#{w = 1\text{--}10}) alongside modal \code{oxcaml.eval_batch} (\code{Py}), reaching \code{22.95 M evals/s} on 2D \code{griewank}, \code{4.29 M evals/s} (\code{20×} vs. Python) on 16D \code{ackley}, and \code{0.23 M evals/s} (\code{210×} vs. Python) on 64D \code{perm-zero}.}
}
